  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "The full paper is present. Some figures are OCR-garbled, but the relevant text and tables are readable.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper discusses representation convergence and proposes it as a hypothesis, but does not measure alignment or convergence between models' representations. Its experiments measure robustness and downstream generalization instead.",
    "gate_note": "The paper says mutual distillation aligns policy output distributions and hypothesizes that representations converge, but reports no direct cross-model representation-similarity result. The rows record its related robustness and frozen-encoder probe findings; the return curves and other generalization comparisons do not measure any claim version.",
    "test_reason": "The paper hypothesizes that mutual distillation makes two RL policies' representations converge, citing the Platonic Representation Hypothesis in a philosophical appendix. It never measures cross-model representation similarity. Its experiments measure each policy's own output invariance to random-CNN renderings (Table 1), linear-probe generalization on frozen encoders (Table 2) and returns. Convergence is also induced on purpose by a KL term between the policies, not measured arising otherwise. No version is tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely correct. The authors conclude that mutual distillation (DML) acts as implicit regularization against irrelevant features, improving robustness and hence generalization, backed by a theoretical bound linking robustness to generalization. Representation convergence between the policies is a stated hypothesis that they do not directly measure.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "The experiments do not use or reproduce the original studies' representation-similarity measurements.",
    "method_sensitivity": "",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "MDPO policies show much lower maximum output change under random-CNN renderings than PPO across eight Procgen games (Table 1). t-SNE plots show more clustered encoder features under random conv and ColorJitter perturbations.",
        "proposed_claim": "Mutual distillation between RL policies yields encoders and policies more invariant to irrelevant visual perturbations.",
